TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A sun-synchronous orbit does not provide a constant supply of sunlight to the spacecraft; it experiences eclipses depending on orbital geometry and season."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The claim that the selected Tasmanian station provides near-polar access frequency is overstated. At about 42°S it is not close to a pole, and access frequency depends on station latitude, orbit geometry, antenna elevation mask, and the analysed time span."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The claim that imaging occurs on the descending part because it is constantly in sunlight is not generally valid. SSO local solar time and ascending/descending illumination must be specified; either branch may be sunlit depending on the orbit."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The orbital-element table does not fully define the initial spacecraft state: for a circular orbit, argument of perigee and true anomaly are undefined, but argument of latitude alone is insufficient without a reference epoch and the remaining element values such as RAAN and inclination."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The report presents Vega E as the selected launch vehicle despite describing it as not yet built, and does not establish that its payload interface, launch date, injection orbit, or rideshare availability suit this mission."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A drag sail can increase atmospheric drag but does not itself guarantee a safe disposal time or complete demise. The report gives no sail area, spacecraft ballistic coefficient, decay-time estimate, or assessment against applicable debris-mitigation limits."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The stated 250 km reef width and 600 km altitude are not connected to sensor swath, ground sampling distance, or revisit/coverage calculations, so the claimed imaging capability is unsubstantiated."}],"overall":"The report proposes a relevant Australian reef-monitoring mission and makes useful use of STK screenshots and access analysis. Its orbit and mission outline are promising, but launch feasibility, imaging performance, communications assumptions, and disposal feasibility are not adequately demonstrated. Presentation and referencing also need improvement; no STK project files are included.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report explains the reef-monitoring mission and its public and scientific rationale, justifies using one spacecraft, and identifies likely users and funding sources. The concept is relevant to Australia and reasonably detailed. However, the operator is not clearly distinguished from customers, and mission need and user roles would benefit from stronger specificity and better-supported references.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The spacecraft is intended to image and send data to ground stations for collation and analysis."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report presents STK-derived orbit views and access results, orbit elements, a launch option, a ground facility, mission duration, and an EOL concept. Important design aspects remain weak or inaccurate: the launch choice is speculative, no STK project files are provided, imaging coverage and resolution are not quantified, and the proposed passive drag-sail deorbit is not analysed. Some operational claims are inconsistent with SSO geometry.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarizes the selected launch, orbit, and ground station and acknowledges uncertainty around Vega E. It is relevant but mostly recaps the proposal rather than drawing detailed conclusions from the analysis; it also does not address important limitations such as imaging performance, contact assumptions, or EOL feasibility.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"After analysis of each mission segment, a fully described satellite configuration has been constructed."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable structure, executive summary, figures, tables, and bibliography. However, several figures and tables lack consistent numbering and captions; the writing contains grammatical and editing errors; and references are incomplete and inconsistently formatted. It cites Wikipedia despite the task explicitly disallowing it, and the bibliography does not provide proper bibliographic details.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
